  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_zk3PT9Rb6zDDRGnY",
   "name": "Acceleration of free fall",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Acceleration of free fall"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In the absence of **air resistance**, all objects fall with the **same *****acceleration***** **regardless of their mass\n- This is called the **acceleration of freefall**\n\n  - This is also sometimes called acceleration due to gravity"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{acceleration} \\mathrm{of} \\mathrm{freefall}=g=9.8m/s^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/02/leaning-tower-of-pisa.png",
   "file": "assets/13c65b1a4b63-leaning-tower-of-pisa.png",
   "alt": "leaning-tower-of-pisa, IGCSE & GCSE Chemistry revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***In the absence of air resistance, Galileo discovered that all objects (near Earth's surface) fall with an acceleration of about 9.8 m/s***<sup>***2***</sup>"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This means that for every second an object falls, its velocity will increase by 9.8 m/s"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- The symbol $g$ also stands for the **gravitational field strength**, and can be used to calculate the force of weight acting an object using its mass:\n\n$W=mg$\n\n- Where:\n\n  - $W$ = the force of weight acting on an object, measured in newtons (N)\n  - $m$ = mass of object, measured in kilograms (kg)\n  - $g$ = gravitational field strength, measured in newtons per kilogram (N/kg)"
  }
 ]
}